      "The train moved slowly out of the station. The city lights slid past the window like slow rain. Dariush could not sleep. Too many thoughts were running through his head. His new job had not worked out. His engagement had ended two months before. He was thirty-one years old, and for the first time in his life, he did not know what came next.",
      "At the next station, a tall man in a grey coat got on. He sat opposite Dariush and smiled politely. He was about sixty, with white hair and tired, kind eyes. He opened a paper bag and took out two sandwiches. Then he did something unexpected. He offered one to Dariush. 'You look like a man who forgot to eat,' he said.",
      "Dariush was surprised, but he was, in fact, terribly hungry. He thanked the man and accepted. They ate in comfortable silence while the train crossed dark fields. Finally, the man introduced himself. His name was Farhad, and he was a carpenter going home after visiting his daughter. 'And you?' he asked. Dariush told him everything. Somehow, it was easier to talk to a stranger.",
      "Farhad listened without interrupting. When Dariush finished, the carpenter was quiet for a while. Then he said, 'You know, when a piece of wood is cracked, young craftsmen throw it away. But an old carpenter knows that the crack is where the light enters. Some of my best work started with broken wood.' Dariush looked at him, not sure he understood.",
      "Farhad explained. Thirty years ago, his workshop had burned down. He had lost everything at the age of thirty. 'I sat in the ashes for three days,' he said. 'Then my father came to me with his old tools and said, you have two hands and thirty years. Start again.' Farhad rebuilt the workshop slowly, piece by piece. Today, his furniture was in houses all over the country.",
